  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study develops and evaluates a safety-data sampling defense for fine-tuning. It cites safety forgetting from benign fine-tuning as motivation, but does not test the listed claims about harmful fine-tuning, token-position concentration, opening-token bypasses, localized parameters, causes of shallowness, or deepening safety.",
    "gate_note": "The study fine-tunes on benign Alpaca data and evaluates the effect of adding selected safe refusal examples. It does not compare the benign-fine-tuned model with its pre-fine-tuning safety behavior, so its results do not establish that benign fine-tuning raised harmful compliance. No results test the listed token, bypass, parameter-localization, or safety-depth claims. The cited claims and results from prior work are not recorded as study results.",
    "test_reason": "This is a mitigation study. It fine-tunes LLaMA 2 7B on 20k Alpaca examples and compares safety-example sampling strategies (SSS-B, PSS-B, Cossim, etc.) against random sampling. Benign-fine-tuning safety forgetting (Qi et al.) is cited only as motivation. The study never compares the fine-tuned model with a pre-fine-tuning, safety-aligned model. The model appears to be base LLaMA 2 7B, following Bianchi et al., not the chat version. So no result shows benign data raising harmful compliance. There are no harmful fine-tuning, per-token KL, prefill/suffix/decoding, parameter-localization, cause-of-shallowness or depth-extending experiments. Adding refusal examples to the training mix is data augmentation, not training that spreads safety beyond the first tokens.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. Small, category-diverse sets of refusal-type safety examples (SSS-B especially) reduce harmfulness after benign fine-tuning with little added data while keeping helpfulness. Larger additions show diminishing returns and more over-rejection. The mechanism is stated as unverified.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study uses LoRA fine-tuning of LLaMA 2 7B on a 20,000-example Alpaca sample, adding selected safety examples; it does not reproduce the harmful-example fine-tuning, KL-position measurement, or opening-token interventions in the cited original sources.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported outcomes vary with safety-example count and sampling strategy; SSS-B comparisons also vary across model architectures. The authors report increasing over-rejection with larger safety-data additions.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Adding category-stratified refusal (T1) safety examples during Alpaca LoRA fine-tuning gives lower harm scores and SALAD-Bench ASR than random safety sampling. Gains are clearest at 100–250 examples and shrink to within-noise at 350–1000. The pattern repeats on LLaMA3, LLaMA3-Instruct, Qwen2.5-Instruct and Mistral at 50/100 examples.",
        "proposed_claim": "Behaviour- and category-aware selection of refusal examples mitigates safety loss during benign fine-tuning more data-efficiently than random safety examples.",
