    "problems": "The study relies on automated safety scorers and classifiers; it reports one main base model and notes use of 10 trials for random baselines. Some deterministic-method results have no confidence intervals. The authors say full human-in-the-loop evaluation is still needed.",
    "rows_left_out": "The tables report additional sampling strategies (Cossim, SSS, PSS, Cossim-B, and PSS-B) across multiple sample sizes. Those strategy-by-size results are not listed here; the retained rows prioritize the proposed SSS-B method, its random comparator, and the cross-model results. Tables 1, 2, and 4.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No fine-tuning on harmful examples; the added data are safe/refusal responses."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "Fine-tunes on benign Alpaca, but there is no measurement of the model before fine-tuning. The base model appears not to be safety-aligned, so no erosion is shown. The no-safety-data scores (2.37 harm score; ~58% ASR, 'estimated') are post-fine-tuning levels only."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "No per-token or position-wise analysis."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "No prefilling or decoding attacks. SALAD-Bench attack-enhanced prompts are used only to compare sampling strategies, not to test bypassing the opening."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "No weight or rank localization."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "No analysis of why alignment is shallow. The authors say the mechanism behind the refusal examples' effect is unverified."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "No depth-extending training or evaluation of whether added safety persists across response positions or attacks."
      }
